.TH "FLOX\-CONTAINERIZE" "1" "" "" "Flox User Manuals"
.SH NAME
flox\-containerize \- export an environment as a container image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
flox [<general\-options>] containerize
     [\-d=<path> | \-r=<owner/name>]
     [\-f=<file>] [\-\-runtime=<runtime>]
     [\-\-tag=<tag>]
     [\-\-label=<key=value>]
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Export a Flox environment as a container image.
The image can be written to a container runtime registry, a file, or
another process.
.PP
\f[B]Note\f[R]: Exporting a container from macOS requires a supported
runtime because a proxy container is used to build the environment and
image.
You may be prompted for permissions to share files into the proxy
container.
Files used in the proxy container are cached using a \f[CR]docker\f[R]
or \f[CR]podman\f[R] volume named \f[CR]flox\-nix\f[R].
It can safely be removed any time a \f[CR]flox containerize\f[R] command
is not running using either \f[CR]docker volume rm flox\-nix\f[R] or
\f[CR]podman volume rm flox\-nix\f[R].
.PP
Running the container will behave like running \f[CR]flox activate\f[R].
Running the container interactively with
\f[CR]docker run \-it <container id>\f[R], will launch a bash subshell
in the container with all your packages and variables set after running
the activation hook.
This is akin to \f[CR]flox activate\f[R].
.PP
Running the container non\-interactively with
\f[CR]docker run <container id>\f[R] allows you to run a command within
the container without launching a subshell, similar to
\f[CR]flox activate \-\-\f[R].
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-file\f[R]
File to write the container image to.
\f[CR]\-\f[R] to write to stdout.
Defaults to \f[CR]{name}\-container.tar\f[R] if \f[CR]\-\-runtime\f[R]
isn\(cqt specified or detected.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-runtime\f[R]
Container runtime to store the image (when \f[CR]\-\-file\f[R] is not
specified) or build the image (when on macOS).
Defaults to detecting the first available on PATH.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-label\f[R]
Set metadata for the container image.
Specify as \f[CR]key=value\f[R] pairs.
Can be specified multiple times.
Works alongside labels defined in the manifest\(cqs
\f[CR][containerize.config]\f[R] section.
.SS Environment Options
If no environment is specified for an environment command, the
environment in the current directory or the active environment that was
last activated is used.
.TP
\f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-dir\f[R]
Path containing a .flox/ directory.
.TP
\f[CR]\-r\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-reference\f[R]
A FloxHub environment, specified in the form \f[CR]<owner>/<name>\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-D\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-default\f[R]
Use your default environment (\f[CR]<your\-user>/default\f[R]).
When unauthenticated in an interactive context, you will be prompted to
log in.
In non\-interactive contexts (e.g., scripts or CI), this flag will fail
with an error when authentication is missing.
.SS General Options
.TP
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R]
Prints help information.
.PP
The following options can be passed when running any \f[CR]flox\f[R]
subcommand but must be specified \f[I]before\f[R] the subcommand.
.TP
\f[CR]\-v\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Increase logging verbosity.
Invoke multiple times for increasing detail.
.TP
\f[CR]\-q\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R]
Silence logs except for errors.
.SH MANIFEST CONFIGURATION
Configuration for the container image produced by
\f[CR]flox containerize\f[R] may be specified in a
\f[CR][containerize.config]\f[R] table in the environment manifest.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Warning:\f[R] \f[CR]containerize.config\f[R] is
\f[B]experimental\f[R], and its behaviour is subject to change
.RE
.PP
The following options from the OCI spec are supported, specified in
\f[CR]kebab\-case\f[R] rather than \f[CR]PascalCase\f[R].
Some options are passed through directly as container image config,
while others are also used at container build time.
See details below.
.IP
.EX
ContainerizeConfig ::= {
  user                      = null | <STRING>
, exposed\-ports             = null | [<STRING>, ...]
, cmd                       = null | [<STRING>, ...]
, volumes                   = null | [<STRING>, ...]
, working\-dir               = null | <STRING>
, labels                    = null | Map[STRING, STRING]
, stop\-signal               = null | <STRING>
}
.EE
.TP
\f[CR]user\f[R]
The username or UID which is a platform\-specific structure that allows
specific control over which user the process run as.
This acts as a default value to use when the value is not specified when
creating a container.
For Linux based systems, all of the following are valid:
\f[CR]user\f[R], \f[CR]uid\f[R], \f[CR]user:group\f[R],
\f[CR]uid:gid\f[R], \f[CR]uid:group\f[R], \f[CR]user:gid\f[R].
If \f[CR]group\f[R]/\f[CR]gid\f[R] is not specified, the default group
and supplementary groups of the given \f[CR]user\f[R]/\f[CR]uid\f[R] in
\f[CR]/etc/passwd\f[R] and \f[CR]/etc/group\f[R] from the container are
applied.
If \f[CR]group\f[R]/\f[CR]gid\f[R] is specified, supplementary groups
from the container are ignored.
This will add an entry to /etc/passwd and /etc/groups inside the
container, so no manual useradd is required.
If both \f[CR]user\f[R] and \f[CR]working\-dir\f[R] are specified,
\f[CR]working\-dir\f[R] will be created with \f[CR]user\f[R] as owner.
.TP
\f[CR]exposed\-ports\f[R]
A set of ports to expose from a container running this image.
Its values can be in the format of: \f[CR]port/tcp\f[R],
\f[CR]port/udp\f[R], \f[CR]port\f[R] with the default protocol being
\f[CR]tcp\f[R] if not specified.
These values act as defaults and are merged with any specified when
creating a container.
.TP
\f[CR]cmd\f[R]
Default arguments to the entrypoint of the container.
These values act as defaults and may be replaced by any specified when
creating a container.
Flox sets an entrypoint to activate the containerized environment, and
\f[CR]cmd\f[R] is then run inside the activation, similar to
\f[CR]flox activate \-c cmd\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]volumes\f[R]
A set of directories describing where the process is likely to write
data specific to a container instance.
.TP
\f[CR]working\-dir\f[R]
Sets the current working directory of the entrypoint process in the
container.
This value acts as a default and may be replaced by a working directory
specified when creating a container.
If both \f[CR]user\f[R] and \f[CR]working\-dir\f[R] are specified,
\f[CR]working\-dir\f[R] will be created with \f[CR]user\f[R] as owner.
.TP
\f[CR]labels\f[R]
This field contains arbitrary metadata for the container.
This property MUST use the \f[B]annotation
rules\f[R] <\f[I]https://github.com/opencontainers/image\-spec/blob/main/annotations.md#rules\f[R]>.
.TP
\f[CR]stop\-signal\f[R]
This field contains the system call signal that will be sent to the
container to exit.
The signal can be a signal name in the format \f[CR]SIGNAME\f[R], for
instance \f[CR]SIGKILL\f[R] or \f[CR]SIGRTMIN+3\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a container image file and load it into Docker:
.IP
.EX
flox containerize \-f ./mycontainer.tar \f[B]&&\f[R] docker load \-i ./mycontainer.tar
.EE
.PP
Load the image into Docker:
.IP
.EX
$ flox containerize \-\-runtime docker

# or through stdout e.g. if \(gadocker\(ga is not in \(gaPATH\(ga:

$ flox containerize \-f \- | /path/to/docker
.EE
.PP
Run the container interactively:
.IP
.EX
$ flox init
$ flox install hello
$ flox containerize \-f \- | docker load
$ docker run \-\-rm \-it <container id>
[floxenv] $ hello
Hello, world!
.EE
.PP
Run a specific command from within the container, but do not launch a
subshell.
.IP
.EX
$ flox init
$ flox install hello
$ flox containerize \-f \- | docker load
$ docker run <container id> hello
Hello, world
.EE
.PP
Create a container with a specific tag:
.IP
.EX
$ flox init
$ flox install hello
$ flox containerize \-\-tag \(aqv1\(aq \-f \- | docker load
$ docker run \-\-rm \-it <container name>:v1
[floxenv] $ hello
Hello, world!
.EE
